[04:53] CANDIDATE: Sure. The one I'm most proud of is Relay. It started as a passion project because I was always walking away from my laptop mid-task and wanted to trigger things from my phone. It's a phone app that sends commands to a small desktop agent over a WebSocket, so I can do things like open a project, run a build or move files without touching the laptop.
[05:27] INTERVIEWER: Sounds like a nice one. Why a WebSocket here specifically, rather than something like polling the desktop agent?
    [item=core-1; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=1137ms wall=1.5s; activity=## Activity (what they did while speaking, and since) | While speaking (25 s): | - -2.0–25.4s pointer on “Chef can perform as many buy-sell transactions as he likes” in the problem (27.4 s) | Since they last spoke (11 s): no typing or drawing. | Right now: caret at line 5, col 1.]
<<READY 05:27>>
[05:27] CANDIDATE: Mainly latency and the fact that the desktop is behind a home router, so the phone can't connect to it directly. Both sides connect out to a small relay server I wrote, first over plain HTTP to authenticate, then they upgrade to a WebSocket. Polling would have meant a delay on every command and a lot of useless requests when nothing is happening. With a socket the server just pushes the command the moment it arrives.
[06:11] INTERVIEWER: Good reasoning on the NAT problem and the push model. On the desktop agent, when a command comes in and fails, how do you handle that — does the phone get feedback that it failed?
    [item=reserve-harder; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=1601ms wall=1.8s; activity=## Activity (what they did while speaking, and since) | While speaking (29 s): | - -2.0–28.8s pointer on “Chef can perform as many buy-sell transactions as he likes” in the problem (30.8 s) | Since they last spoke (6 s): no typing or drawing. | Right now: caret at line 5, col 1.]
<<READY 06:11>>
[06:11] CANDIDATE: Right now the desktop agent sends back a small JSON result with a status and an error message, and the phone shows it as a toast. I haven't built retries, so if the socket drops mid-command the phone just doesn't know. Also, if I resend after reconnecting, something like a move-files command could run twice, so I really should add command IDs to make it idempotent. That's on my list.
[06:52] INTERVIEWER: Good that you see the gap — idempotency with command IDs is the right instinct. Keep it in mind for later. Now, let's move on to a new problem.
